    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you expand a bit more on the intermediate stages after the glycosylamine formation? Specifically, I'm looking for more detail on the Amadori rearrangement and how the subsequent fragmentation of these intermediates leads to the formation of melanoidins. I want to make sure I'm describing the molecular pathway accurately for my thesis.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Following glycosylamine formation, the Amadori rearrangement occurs, in which the N-glycosyl compound rearranges into a ketosamine (or Heyns rearrangement). H+ ions facilitate the migration of the alkyl or aryl group from the amino nitrogen to the adjacent carbonyl oxygen. This rearrangement is a crucial step because it stabilizes the intermediate while setting the stage for further interaction.\n\n\nSubsequent fragmentation of these Amadori rearrangement products, under alkaline conditions, leads to the formation of different carbonyl compounds like dicarbonyls and di- and tricarbonyl compounds. These carbonyl intermediates then participate in condensation reactions, as they are highly reactive due to their stability resulting from double bonds. The condensation creates a complex, high-molecular weight structure which eventually forms brown or black Melanoidins. These melanoidins contain numerous aromatic rings and contribute to the rich flavors and brown colors observed in Maillard reactions, especially in cooked, protein-heavy foods.",
      "source": "target",
